'''''Archontophoenix cunninghamiana''''' – commonly known as '''Bangalow palm''', '''king palm''', '''Illawarra palm''' or '''''piccabeen palm''''' – is a tree in the palm family [[Arecaceae]], which is [[endemism|endemic]] to the east coast of [[New South Wales]] and [[Queensland]], Australia.

==Description==
The Bangalow palm has a single trunk growing up to {{cvt|30|m}} tall and a [[Diameter at breast height |diameter]] of {{cvt|30|cm}}.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA}} The trunk is swollen at the base and is marked by prominent leaf scars at regular intervals along its length.{{R|RBGS|RFK}} The [[crownshaft]] is around {{cvt|140|cm}} long and is green or purple green.{{R|RFK|FOA}} Around 9 to 12 leaves (or fronds) make up the crown, each about {{cvt|4|-|4.5|m}} long and with around 90 pairs of leaflets.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA}} The fronds usually have a half-twist so that the leaflets at the distal end are more or less vertical. The leaflets measure up to {{cvt|100|cm}} long and {{cvt|10|cm}} wide.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA}}

The [[inflorescence]] is a much-branched [[panicle]] arising from the base of the crownshaft and measuring up to {{cvt|150|cm}} long.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA}} The flowers are pink to lilac or purple; the [[staminate]] (functionally male) flowers measure up to {{cvt|6|mm}} long and the [[pistillate]] (functionally female) flowers are around {{cvt|5|mm}} long.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA}}

The [[globose]] fruit is a [[drupe]] to around {{cvt|10|-|15|mm|1}} diameter. They are bright red when ripe and contain a single seed.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA}}

==Taxonomy==
This species was first described in 1858 by the German botanist [[Hermann Wendland]], who gave it the name ''Ptychosperma cunninghamianum''.{{R|APNI}} Seventeen years later, working with his countryman [[Carl Georg Oscar Drude]], Wendland reviewed his description and gave the species the current combination. Their work was published in the journal ''Linnaea: ein Journal für die Botanik in ihrem ganzen Umfange, oder Beiträge zur Pflanzenkunde'' in 1875.{{R|APNI|POWO}}

===Etymology===
The genus name is derived from the Ancient Greek [[wikt:ἄρχων#Ancient Greek|''ἄρχων'']]  (''árkhōn''), meaning 'chieftain' or 'ruler', combined with the palm genus [[Phoenix (plant)|Phoenix]]. It was created by Wendland and Drude and refers to their perceived 'regal stature' of these palms. The [[Botanical name#Binary name|species epithet]] ''cunninghamiana'' was coined by Wendland to honour the English botanist [[Allan Cunningham (botanist)|Allan Cunningham]].{{R|COOPER2}}

==Distribution==
The native range of the Bangalow palm is from [[Mount Elliot, Queensland|Mount Elliot]], near [[Townsville]] in tropical Queensland, southwards in coastal and sub-coastal areas to [[Bateman's Bay]] in southern New South Wales.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA}} It grows in [[rainforest]] and [[Sclerophyll#Australian bush|wet scleophyll]] forest, in swampy areas and beside rivers and creeks, at altitudes ranging from sea level to {{cvt|1200|m|-1}}.{{R|RBGS|RFK|FOA|COOPER2}}

===Origin===
The genus ''Archontophoenix'' belongs to the subtribe [[Archontophoenicinae]], which is distributed between eastern Australia, New Zealand, New Caledonia, [[Lord Howe Island]] and [[Norfolk Island]], and it is believed that this region is where the all the included species originated, including ''A.&nbsp;cunninghamiana''. This is supported by the lack of any close relatives of the group in the regions to the north of Australia, e.g. Indonesia.{{R|DOWE|DOMENECH}}

==Invasive potential==
It has become a noxious weed in many areas where it has been used as an [[ornamental plant]].
In southern Brazil, it has become an [[invasive species]], and it has been suggested that it benefits from the earlier local extinction of the native palm ''[[Euterpe edulis]]''.{{R|CHRISTIANINI}}  

In New Zealand, ''A. cunninghamiana'' could invade native forests, since it has the same ecological requirements as the native [[nīkau|nīkau palm]].{{R|ARPS}} The [[Department of Conservation (New Zealand)]] has included ''A. cunninghamiana'' in its List of Environmental Weeds in New Zealand 2024.<ref name="List of Environmental weeds in New Zealand 2024">{{cite web |last1=McAlpine |first1=Kate G. |last2=Howell |first2=Clayson J. |title=Rapid growth for weeds list |url=https://www.doc.govt.nz/news/media-releases/2024-media-releases/rapid-growth-for-weeds-list/ |website=Department of Concervation |access-date=21 August 2024 |ref=Kate G. McAlpine and Clayson J. Howell. Science for Conservation 340. 37 p.}}</ref> The [[Auckland Council]] has declared ''A. cunninghamiana'' to be a pest species, and has introduced restrictions on the movement of the plant within the Hauraki Gulf Controlled Area.{{R|HGCA}}

In the United States, the palm is commonly cultivated in California from San Luis Obispo south to the Mexican border and in much of central and southern Florida.{{cn|date=May 2023}}

